@*
    One empire in a list, as the game lists one.

    The game's entry is in interface/select_empire_design.gui — 200 by 65, a clipped close-up of the
    ruler's face at the left, then three lines stacked beside it at (65,6), (65,24) and (65,42): the
    empire's name, the government it turned out to be, and the species class. Followed here, three
    lines and all. The one departure is the flag, which the game's own entry does not carry.

    Both lists on the front page use this, so "the same card" means the same component rather than
    two that resemble each other.
*@

@*
    The card and its share button are siblings in a wrapper rather than one inside the other, because
    a button may not contain a button — nested, the browser closes the outer one early and the card
    comes apart. The wrapper is what the grid lays out, so the card keeps its full width inside it.
*@
<div class="sem-empire-slot">
    <button type="button" class="sem-empire @(Selected ? "selected" : null) @(Broken ? "broken" : null)"
            disabled="@Disabled" @onclick="OnClick">

        @if (Flag is not null)
        {
            <FlagPreview Flag="@Flag" Class="small" />
        }

        @if (Portrait is { Length: > 0 } face)
        {
            <img class="face" src="@Session.Data.AssetUrl(face)" alt="" loading="lazy">
        }

        <span class="body">
            <span class="name">@Name</span>
            <span class="sub">@Government</span>

            @if (SpeciesClass is { Length: > 0 } kind)
            {
                <span class="sub">@kind</span>
            }
        </span>
    </button>

    @*
        Offered on the card so a link can be taken without opening the empire first, which was the
        only way to reach one. Hidden until the pointer is over the card or something inside it has
        focus — a share button on every card at rest would put a second thing to click on a list
        whose whole job is choosing between fifty-two of them.
    *@
    @if (OnCopyLink.HasDelegate && !Disabled)
    {
        <button type="button" class="icon sem-empire-share" title="@CopyTitle"
                aria-label="@($"Copy a link to {Name}")" @onclick="OnCopyLink">
            <ActionIcon Name="share" />
        </button>
    }

    @*
        Something to take hold of. The card itself is a button, and a browser will not begin a drag
        from inside one - which is why dragging the card did nothing at all: there was nowhere on it
        to grab.

        A button rather than a bare handle, so it is in the tab order like everything else here: the
        arrow keys move the card once it has focus, which is the whole of what the two arrow buttons
        did and none of the space they took.
    *@
    @if (OnMove.HasDelegate)
    {
        <button type="button" class="sem-empire-grip" data-grip
                title="@($"Drag to move {Name}, or use the arrow keys")"
                aria-label="@($"Move {Name}. Drag, or press the up and down arrow keys.")"
                @onkeydown="Nudge">⠿</button>
    }
</div>

@code {
    [CascadingParameter]
    public required DesignSession Session { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Called with -1 or 1 to move this empire one place that way.</summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// A direction rather than a position, because the card does not know where it sits and the
    /// list it belongs to does.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback<int> OnMove { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Moves the card with the arrow keys, for anyone who cannot drag one.</summary>
    private Task Nudge(KeyboardEventArgs key) => key.Key switch
    {
        "ArrowUp" or "ArrowLeft" => OnMove.InvokeAsync(-1),
        "ArrowDown" or "ArrowRight" => OnMove.InvokeAsync(1),
        _ => Task.CompletedTask,
    };

    /// <summary>The empire's flag, drawn as the game draws one.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EmpireFlag? Flag { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The ruler's likeness, already drawn.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Portrait { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What the empire is called.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required string Name { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What the game would call its government, such as "Representative Democracy".</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Government { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// What kind of thing lives there, such as "Humanoid". The game's own third line.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? SpeciesClass { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public bool Selected { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the game would refuse this empire.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Marked rather than counted. The card carries four things and a fifth line saying "2 problems"
    /// was the one the list could least afford, but it was also the only place a player learned an
    /// empire was broken without opening it — so the fact stays and only the sentence goes.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Broken { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Whether there is nothing to show, as for a built-in empire stored unreadably.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Disabled { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback OnClick { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Copies a link to this empire. Where nothing is given, the card has no share button.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public EventCallback OnCopyLink { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// What the share button says, which the host varies to report whether the last press worked.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string CopyTitle { get; set; } = "Copy a link to this empire";
}
